// Longer, DK-style material for Health and the Body articles (part 2): a lead, extra sections, key facts and key dates.
window.DEEP=window.DEEP||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP,{
ear:{l:"The ears are the organs of hearing and balance. They collect sound waves from the air, turn them into nerve signals the brain can understand and, deep inside the skull, sense the position and movement of the head so that we can stand, walk and run without falling over.",
s:[["How sound travels","Sound is a vibration that travels through the air as waves. The outer ear funnels these waves down the ear canal to the eardrum, a thin membrane that vibrates. The three tiny middle-ear bones, the hammer, anvil and stirrup, amplify the vibrations and pass them to the inner ear."],
["Inside the cochlea","The cochlea is a snail-shaped tube filled with fluid and lined with about 15,000 hair cells. Vibrations make waves in the fluid that bend the hairs, which send signals along the auditory nerve. Hair cells near the entrance respond to high-pitched sounds and those deep inside to low ones."],
["Pitch and loudness","Pitch depends on frequency, measured in hertz. Young people can hear from about 20 to 20,000 hertz, but the upper limit falls with age. Loudness is measured in decibels. Sounds above about 85 decibels, such as heavy traffic, can harm hearing over time, and sounds above 120 decibels can cause pain."],
["Hearing loss and deafness","Hearing loss can be caused by aging, loud noise, infections or genes. Hearing aids make sounds louder, and cochlear implants, first used in the 1970s and 1980s, send electrical signals directly to the auditory nerve. Many deaf people communicate using sign languages, such as American, British or Japanese Sign Language."]],
k:[["Main parts","Outer, middle and inner ear"],["Smallest bone","Stapes (stirrup), about 3 mm"],["Hearing range","About 20 to 20,000 Hz"],["Hair cells","About 15,000 per cochlea"],["Balance organs","Semicircular canals and vestibule"]],
d:[["1543","Vesalius describes two of the middle-ear bones"],["1561","Fallopius describes the cochlea"],["1851","Corti describes the hearing organ"],["1898","First electric hearing aid"],["1961","Békésy wins the Nobel Prize for hearing research"],["1984","Cochlear implants approved for adults in the US"]]},
teeth:{l:"Teeth are hard structures in the mouth used for biting and chewing food, and they also help us speak clearly and shape our smile. Humans grow two sets of teeth in a lifetime, and with good care, the second, permanent set can last for life.",
s:[["Kinds of teeth","Different teeth do different jobs. Sharp incisors at the front cut and bite. Pointed canines tear food. Premolars and molars at the back, with broad, bumpy surfaces, crush and grind it. The last molars, called wisdom teeth, often appear between ages 17 and 25, and some people never get them."],
["Inside a tooth","Under the hard enamel is dentin, a bony layer that makes up most of the tooth. At the center is the pulp, which holds nerves and blood vessels. The root anchors the tooth in the jawbone and is covered by cementum. Enamel cannot repair itself once it is badly damaged."],
["Gum disease","If plaque is not removed, it hardens into tartar and irritates the gums, causing gingivitis, with red, bleeding gums. Without treatment it can become periodontitis, which damages the bone holding teeth in place and is a leading cause of tooth loss in adults."],
["Dentistry through history","People in ancient times drilled teeth with bows, and the Maya decorated teeth with jade inlays. In the 1700s Pierre Fauchard, called the father of modern dentistry, described fillings and braces. Fluoride toothpaste, introduced widely in the 1950s, greatly reduced tooth decay."]],
k:[["Baby teeth","20"],["Adult teeth","32 (including wisdom teeth)"],["Hardest substance","Enamel"],["Main layers","Enamel, dentin, pulp"],["Brushing","Twice a day with fluoride toothpaste"]],
d:[["c. 7000 BCE","Evidence of tooth drilling in Pakistan"],["1728","Fauchard’s book on dentistry"],["1780","Modern toothbrush mass-produced in England"],["1840","First dental college opens in Baltimore"],["1945","Community water fluoridation begins"],["1955","Fluoride toothpaste marketed widely"]]},
water:{l:"Water is essential to life. Every cell, tissue and organ in the body needs it to work properly. A person can survive for weeks without food but only a few days without water, which is why drinking enough clean water every day is one of the simplest ways to stay healthy.",
s:[["What water does","Water makes up most of the blood, which carries oxygen and nutrients. It cushions the brain and spinal cord, lubricates joints and helps form saliva for digestion. When the body is hot, it releases water as sweat, which cools the skin as it evaporates."],
["Where water goes","An adult loses roughly 2 to 3 liters of water a day through urine, sweat, breath and the gut, and more in hot weather or during exercise. People in hot climates can lose a lot of water through sweating, so it is important to drink regularly."],
["Signs of dehydration","Early signs include thirst, dark yellow urine, a dry mouth, tiredness and headaches. Severe dehydration can cause dizziness, confusion and a fast heartbeat, and it is dangerous, especially for babies and older people. Diarrhea is a major cause of dehydration in children, treated with oral rehydration solution."],
["Clean drinking water","Unsafe water can carry germs that cause diarrhea, cholera and typhoid. Boiling water, filtering it or adding chlorine kills most germs. Around the world about 2 billion people still lack safely managed drinking water at home. The United Nations recognized access to clean water as a human right in 2010."]],
k:[["Body water","About 60% of adult body weight"],["Daily loss","About 2–3 liters"],["Survival without water","About 3 days"],["Water in the brain","About 75%"],["Rehydration treatment","Oral rehydration solution"]],
d:[["1854","John Snow links cholera to a water pump"],["1908","Chlorination of public water begins in the US"],["1960s","Oral rehydration therapy developed"],["1978","The Lancet calls oral rehydration a great medical advance"],["2010","UN declares water a human right"],["2015","Clean water becomes a Sustainable Development Goal"]]},
flu:{l:"Colds and influenza, or flu, are common infections of the nose, throat and lungs caused by viruses. Colds are usually mild, but flu can be serious and even deadly, especially for the very young, the elderly and people with other illnesses, and new flu strains can spread around the world.",
s:[["Cold or flu?","A cold usually starts slowly, with a sore throat, sneezing and a runny nose, and lasts about a week. Flu often strikes suddenly with high fever, chills, aching muscles, headache and great tiredness. Flu is more likely to lead to complications such as pneumonia."],
["How they spread","Cold and flu viruses spread in tiny droplets when infected people cough, sneeze or talk, and by touching surfaces and then the eyes, nose or mouth. In temperate countries flu peaks in winter. In the tropics, flu cases often rise during the rainy season."],
["Treatment","Most people recover with rest, fluids and medicines that ease symptoms such as fever. Antiviral drugs can shorten flu if taken early, but antibiotics do not help because they only kill bacteria. People should see a doctor if they have trouble breathing or symptoms that keep getting worse."],
["Pandemics","When a new flu strain appears that people have no immunity to, it can spread worldwide as a pandemic. Besides 1918, flu pandemics occurred in 1957, 1968 and 2009. Scientists watch birds and pigs closely, because new flu strains often come from animals."]],
k:[["Cold viruses","More than 200 types, mainly rhinoviruses"],["Flu virus types","A, B, C and D"],["Deadliest pandemic","1918 flu, about 50 million deaths"],["Prevention","Yearly flu vaccine, handwashing"],["Antibiotics","Do not work against viruses"]],
d:[["1918","Great influenza pandemic"],["1933","Human flu virus first isolated"],["1945","First flu vaccine approved for civilians"],["1957","Asian flu pandemic"],["1968","Hong Kong flu pandemic"],["2009","H1N1 swine flu pandemic"]]},
kidney:{l:"The kidneys are two bean-shaped organs that clean the blood, removing wastes and extra water to make urine. Working day and night, they filter all of the body’s blood many times each day and keep its water, salts and acids in careful balance.",
s:[["Where they are","The kidneys sit on either side of the spine, just below the ribs, toward the back. Each is about 11 cm long. Urine flows from each kidney down a tube called a ureter to the bladder, which stores it until it is passed out through the urethra."],
["How a nephron works","Each nephron has a tiny ball of capillaries called a glomerulus, which filters water, salts, sugar and waste from the blood. As the fluid travels along a long, looped tube, the body takes back almost all the water and useful substances. What is left, mostly water and urea, becomes urine."],
["More than filters","The kidneys also make hormones. One, erythropoietin, tells the bone marrow to make red blood cells. Another helps control blood pressure. The kidneys also turn vitamin D into its active form, which the body needs for strong bones."],
["Kidney disease worldwide","Chronic kidney disease affects about one in ten adults worldwide and is rising, driven largely by diabetes and high blood pressure. Many people do not know they have it until their kidneys are badly damaged. Millions depend on regular dialysis, and in many poorer countries most people who need it cannot get it."]],
k:[["Number","2"],["Size","About 11 cm long each"],["Nephrons","About 1 million per kidney"],["Blood filtered","About 180 liters a day"],["Urine produced","About 1–2 liters a day"]],
d:[["1842","William Bowman describes the nephron capsule"],["1943","Willem Kolff builds the first artificial kidney"],["1954","First successful kidney transplant"],["1960","Long-term dialysis becomes possible"],["1983","The drug ciclosporin is approved, making transplants far more successful"],["2006","World Kidney Day first held"]]},
hormone:{l:"Hormones are chemical messengers made by glands and released into the bloodstream. Traveling throughout the body, they control growth, energy use, mood, sleep, hunger and how the body responds to stress, acting as a slow but powerful partner to the nervous system.",
s:[["The endocrine system","The glands that make hormones form the endocrine system. The pituitary gland, the size of a pea at the base of the brain, is called the master gland because it controls many others. The thyroid in the neck sets the body’s energy use, the adrenal glands above the kidneys release stress hormones, and the pancreas controls blood sugar."],
["Lock and key","Each hormone fits receptors on particular target cells, like a key in a lock. When a hormone attaches, it triggers changes inside the cell. Feedback loops keep hormone levels balanced: when there is enough of a hormone, the gland that makes it slows down."],
["Fight or flight","When you are frightened, the adrenal glands release adrenaline. Within seconds the heart races, breathing speeds up, the pupils widen and stored sugar is released for energy, preparing the body to fight or run. The stress hormone cortisol helps the body cope with longer-lasting stress."],
["Diabetes","In diabetes, the body cannot control blood sugar properly. In type 1 the pancreas makes little or no insulin, and patients need insulin injections. In type 2, the more common form, the body does not respond well to insulin. Healthy eating, exercise and medicines help manage it. More than 500 million adults worldwide have diabetes."]],
k:[["System","Endocrine system"],["Master gland","Pituitary"],["Blood sugar hormone","Insulin (from the pancreas)"],["Stress hormones","Adrenaline, cortisol"],["Word origin","Greek hormon, to set in motion"]],
d:[["1902","Secretin discovered"],["1905","Ernest Starling coins the word hormone"],["1921","Insulin discovered"],["1922","First patient treated with insulin"],["1950","Nobel Prize for cortisone research"],["1982","First genetically engineered human insulin"]]},
hygiene:{l:"Hygiene is the set of everyday habits, such as washing hands, bathing, cleaning teeth and keeping food and water clean, that help prevent the spread of germs and disease. Simple handwashing with soap is one of the most effective ways to protect health ever discovered.",
s:[["Ignored warnings","Semmelweis noticed that women in a ward run by doctors, who came straight from performing autopsies, died of fever far more often than those in a ward run by midwives. Although handwashing cut deaths dramatically, many doctors rejected his ideas, and he died in 1865 without recognition."],
["Lister and clean surgery","Inspired by Pasteur, the British surgeon Joseph Lister began using carbolic acid to clean wounds, instruments and hands in 1867. Deaths after surgery fell sharply. Antiseptic, and later sterile, methods transformed hospitals."],
["How to wash hands","Wet your hands with clean water and apply soap. Rub palms, backs, between the fingers, the thumbs, fingertips and wrists for at least 20 seconds, about as long as singing Happy Birthday twice. Rinse and dry with a clean towel. Soap works by breaking apart the fatty outer layer of many germs."],
["Food hygiene","Keeping food safe prevents food poisoning. The main rules are to keep hands and kitchen clean, separate raw meat from cooked food, cook food thoroughly and store it at safe temperatures. Restaurants, canteens and street food stalls are inspected by health officials in many countries."]],
k:[["Pioneer","Ignaz Semmelweis, 1847"],["Antiseptic surgery","Joseph Lister, 1867"],["Handwashing time","At least 20 seconds"],["Global Handwashing Day","15 October (since 2008)"],["Word origin","Hygieia, Greek goddess of health"]],
d:[["1847","Semmelweis introduces handwashing"],["1854","John Snow traces cholera"],["1858","London’s Great Stink spurs sewer building"],["1867","Lister’s antiseptic surgery"],["2008","First Global Handwashing Day"],["2020","Handwashing campaigns during COVID-19"]]},
malaria:{l:"Malaria is a serious disease caused by a tiny parasite spread by mosquitoes. It causes fever, chills and sickness and can kill if not treated quickly. Although it has been wiped out in many countries, it remains one of the world’s deadliest diseases, especially for young children in Africa.",
s:[["The parasite’s life cycle","When an infected female Anopheles mosquito bites, Plasmodium parasites enter the blood and travel to the liver, where they multiply. They then burst out and invade red blood cells, multiplying again and destroying the cells. When another mosquito bites, it picks up parasites and carries them to the next person."],
["Symptoms","Malaria usually begins one to four weeks after a bite with fever, shaking chills, sweating, headache and body aches. The fevers may come in cycles as parasites burst from red blood cells. Severe malaria can cause anemia, breathing problems, organ failure and coma."],
["Countries that beat malaria","More than 40 countries and territories have been certified malaria-free by the World Health Organization. They include Sri Lanka in 2016, China and El Salvador in 2021, and Egypt and Cabo Verde in 2024. Bed nets treated with insecticide, indoor spraying and quick testing and treatment were the main tools, along with strong health systems."],
["The fight continues","The name malaria comes from the Italian mala aria, meaning bad air, from the old belief that swamp air caused it. Countries such as China, El Salvador and Sri Lanka have been certified malaria-free. Drug resistance and insecticide resistance remain challenges."]],
k:[["Cause","Plasmodium parasites"],["Spread by","Female Anopheles mosquitoes"],["Deaths per year","About 600,000"],["Main treatment","Artemisinin-based combination therapy"],["Word origin","Italian mala aria, bad air"]],
d:[["1820","Quinine isolated from cinchona bark"],["1880","Laveran sees the parasite in blood"],["1897","Ross shows mosquitoes spread malaria"],["1972","Tu Youyou’s team isolates artemisinin"],["2021","WHO recommends the first malaria vaccine"],["2021","China certified malaria-free"]]},
allergy:{l:"An allergy is a reaction of the immune system to a substance that is normally harmless, such as pollen, dust, pet hair or certain foods. Allergies are very common, ranging from a runny nose and itchy eyes to rare but life-threatening reactions.",
s:[["What triggers allergies","Substances that cause allergies are called allergens. Common ones include pollen from grasses and trees, dust mites, mold, pet dander, insect stings, some medicines and foods such as peanuts, shellfish, eggs, milk and wheat."],
["How the reaction happens","The first time a person meets an allergen, their immune system may make antibodies called IgE against it. Next time, the allergen links to these antibodies on mast cells, which burst open and release histamine and other chemicals. These cause swelling, itching, sneezing and mucus."],
["Kinds of allergic illness","Hay fever, or allergic rhinitis, causes sneezing and a runny nose. Asthma can be triggered by allergies and narrows the airways. Eczema causes itchy, inflamed skin, and hives are raised, itchy bumps. Food allergies can affect the skin, gut and breathing."],
["Testing and treatment","Doctors can test for allergies with skin-prick tests or blood tests. Avoiding the allergen is the main treatment. Antihistamines and nasal sprays ease symptoms, and allergy shots, or immunotherapy, gradually train the immune system to tolerate some allergens. People with severe allergies may carry an epinephrine auto-injector."]],
k:[["Cause","Immune system overreaction"],["Key chemical","Histamine"],["Antibody involved","IgE"],["Emergency treatment","Epinephrine (adrenaline)"],["Common forms","Hay fever, asthma, eczema, food allergy"]],
d:[["1819","John Bostock describes hay fever"],["1906","Clemens von Pirquet coins the word allergy"],["1911","First allergy shots tested"],["1937","First antihistamine developed"],["1967","IgE antibody discovered"],["1987","Epinephrine auto-injector approved for public use"]]},
spine:{l:"The spine, or backbone, is a column of bones running down the middle of the back. It supports the head and body, lets us bend and twist and protects the spinal cord, the main pathway of nerves between the brain and the rest of the body.",
s:[["Regions of the spine","The spine has five regions. Seven cervical vertebrae form the neck, 12 thoracic vertebrae hold the ribs, and five large lumbar vertebrae in the lower back bear the most weight. Below them, five fused vertebrae form the sacrum, and a few tiny ones form the tailbone, or coccyx."],
["Discs and joints","Between the vertebrae are discs with a tough outer ring and a soft, jelly-like center. They act as shock absorbers and allow the spine to bend. Small joints at the back of each vertebra guide movement. Together, the many small movements let the spine curve and twist."],
["The spinal cord","The spinal cord is about 45 cm long and as thick as a finger. It runs through a channel formed by holes in the vertebrae. Pairs of spinal nerves branch out between the vertebrae to every part of the body. Injury to the spinal cord can cause paralysis below the point of damage."],
["Good posture","Good posture means keeping the spine in its natural curves. When sitting, keep your feet flat, your back supported and screens at eye level. When lifting, bend your knees and keep the load close to your body. Heavy school bags should be carried on both shoulders."]],
k:[["Vertebrae","33 (some fused)"],["Regions","Cervical, thoracic, lumbar, sacrum, coccyx"],["Neck vertebrae","7 (same as a giraffe)"],["Spinal cord length","About 45 cm"],["Natural curves","4"]],
d:[["c. 1600 BCE","Egyptian papyrus describes spinal injuries"],["1543","Vesalius illustrates the spine"],["1895","X-rays allow doctors to see the spine"],["1934","Herniated disc linked to sciatica"],["1960s","Spinal surgery techniques advance"],["2010s","Electrical stimulation helps some paralyzed patients walk"]]}
});

