/* data/deep-math.js */
// Longer, DK-style material for Mathematics articles: a lead, extra sections, key facts and key dates.
window.DEEP=window.DEEP||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP,{
zero:{l:"Numbers are the symbols and words we use to count and measure, and zero is perhaps the most important number ever invented. Without zero, the place-value system we use to write large numbers, and all of modern mathematics, science and computing, would be impossible.",
s:[["Place value","In our number system the position of a digit tells its value: in 305, the 3 means three hundreds. Zero holds an empty place, so 305 cannot be confused with 35. Systems without a zero, such as Roman numerals, make large numbers and arithmetic clumsy."],
["Kinds of numbers","Natural numbers are the counting numbers 1, 2, 3 and so on. Adding zero and negative numbers gives the integers. Fractions and decimals that can be written as a ratio are rational numbers, while numbers such as pi and the square root of 2 are irrational. Together they form the real numbers."],
["Negative numbers","Chinese mathematicians used red and black counting rods for positive and negative numbers more than 2,000 years ago, and Brahmagupta described rules for debts and fortunes. European mathematicians were suspicious of negative numbers until the 1600s, calling them absurd."],
["Zero in computers","Computers use the binary system, which has only two digits, 0 and 1. Every number, letter, picture and sound in a computer is stored as long strings of zeros and ones, switched off and on by tiny transistors."]],
k:[["Our numerals","Hindu–Arabic, base 10"],["Zero as a number","Brahmagupta, 628 CE"],["Babylonian base","60"],["Maya base","20"],["Computer base","2 (binary)"]],
d:[["c. 3000 BCE","Egyptian and Sumerian number systems"],["c. 300 BCE","Babylonian placeholder zero"],["c. 350 CE","Maya use a shell symbol for zero"],["628","Brahmagupta’s rules for zero"],["c. 825","Al-Khwarizmi writes on Hindu numerals"],["1202","Fibonacci’s Liber Abaci"]]},
euclid:{l:"Geometry is the branch of mathematics that studies shapes, sizes, angles and space. Euclid, a Greek mathematician who worked in Alexandria, Egypt, gathered and organized the geometry of his time into the Elements, one of the most influential books ever written.",
s:[["Axioms and proofs","Euclid began with definitions, such as a point is that which has no part, and five postulates, or basic assumptions, such as a straight line can be drawn between any two points. From these he proved 465 propositions, each building on earlier ones, a method still used in mathematics today."],
["Shapes and angles","Plane geometry studies flat shapes such as triangles, squares and circles. The angles inside any triangle add up to 180 degrees. Solid geometry studies three-dimensional shapes such as cubes, spheres and pyramids. Euclid showed there are exactly five regular solids, the Platonic solids."],
["Measuring the Earth","Around 240 BCE Eratosthenes, librarian at Alexandria, used geometry to measure Earth. He compared the angle of the Sun’s shadows at noon in two cities a known distance apart and calculated a circumference close to the true value of about 40,000 km."],
["Geometry everywhere","Builders, architects and surveyors use geometry to measure land and design buildings. Map makers, artists, video game designers and GPS systems all rely on it. Coordinate geometry, invented by René Descartes, links shapes to algebra using x and y axes."]],
k:[["Author","Euclid of Alexandria"],["Book","The Elements, 13 books"],["Date","c. 300 BCE"],["Propositions","465"],["Triangle angles","Add up to 180°"]],
d:[["c. 1800 BCE","Babylonians know Pythagorean triples"],["c. 530 BCE","Pythagoras’ school in Croton"],["c. 300 BCE","Euclid’s Elements"],["c. 240 BCE","Eratosthenes measures Earth"],["1482","First printed edition of the Elements"],["1637","Descartes invents coordinate geometry"]]},
prob:{l:"Probability is the mathematics of chance. It measures how likely something is to happen, from the toss of a coin to the chance of rain tomorrow, and it helps people make sensible decisions when the future is uncertain.",
s:[["Calculating chances","When all outcomes are equally likely, probability is the number of ways an event can happen divided by the total number of outcomes. A fair die has six faces, so the chance of rolling a 4 is 1 in 6. The chance of tossing two heads in a row is ½ × ½, or ¼."],
["Expected value","Expected value is the average result you would get if you repeated an experiment many times. Lotteries and casinos are designed so that players’ expected value is negative: over time, the house always wins. Understanding this helps people avoid costly gambling."],
["The gambler’s fallacy","Many people believe that after a run of heads, tails is due. But a fair coin has no memory, and each toss still has a 50% chance. This mistaken belief is called the gambler’s fallacy, and it has cost gamblers fortunes."],
["Surprising results","Probability can be surprising. In a group of just 23 people, there is better than an even chance that two share a birthday. In the Monty Hall puzzle, switching doors doubles a contestant’s chance of winning, a result many people refuse to believe."]],
k:[["Scale","0 (impossible) to 1 (certain)"],["Founders","Pascal and Fermat, 1654"],["Coin toss","1/2 for heads"],["Die roll","1/6 for each face"],["Birthday paradox","23 people, over 50% chance"]],
d:[["1564","Cardano writes a book on games of chance"],["1654","Pascal–Fermat letters"],["1657","Huygens publishes on probability"],["1713","Bernoulli’s law of large numbers"],["1812","Laplace’s great work on probability"],["1933","Kolmogorov’s axioms"]]},
prime:{l:"Prime numbers are whole numbers greater than 1 that can be divided evenly only by 1 and themselves. They are the building blocks of all whole numbers, and despite thousands of years of study, they still hold some of the deepest unsolved mysteries in mathematics.",
s:[["Building blocks","Every whole number greater than 1 is either prime or can be written as a product of primes in exactly one way, ignoring order. For example, 60 = 2 × 2 × 3 × 5. This fact is called the fundamental theorem of arithmetic. Numbers that are not prime are called composite."],
["The sieve of Eratosthenes","A simple way to find primes is to list numbers and cross out the multiples of 2, then of 3, then of 5 and so on. The numbers left uncrossed are prime. This method was described by Eratosthenes over 2,000 years ago."],
["The largest known primes","The largest known primes are Mersenne primes, of the form 2 raised to a power, minus 1. Volunteers around the world search for them with their computers. In 2024 a new record prime was found with more than 41 million digits."],
["Unsolved puzzles","Goldbach’s conjecture, proposed in 1742, says every even number greater than 2 is the sum of two primes, such as 10 = 3 + 7. It has been checked for enormous numbers but never proved. The Riemann hypothesis, about how primes are spread out, carries a million-dollar prize."]],
k:[["Smallest prime","2 (the only even prime)"],["Primes below 100","25"],["Infinitely many","Proved by Euclid"],["Record prime (2024)","Over 41 million digits"],["Famous problem","Riemann hypothesis"]],
d:[["c. 300 BCE","Euclid proves infinitely many primes"],["c. 240 BCE","Sieve of Eratosthenes"],["1742","Goldbach’s conjecture"],["1859","Riemann’s hypothesis"],["1996","Great Internet Mersenne Prime Search begins"],["2024","Record prime discovered"]]},
calc:{l:"Calculus is the mathematics of change and motion. It gives precise tools for describing how things grow, shrink, speed up and slow down, and for adding up countless tiny pieces. Invented in the 1600s, it became the language of physics and engineering.",
s:[["Ancient roots","Long before calculus, Archimedes found areas and volumes by slicing shapes into ever thinner pieces, a method of exhaustion. Mathematicians in Kerala, India, in the 1300s to 1500s, such as Madhava, discovered infinite series for pi and trigonometry that anticipated parts of calculus."],
["Derivatives","A derivative measures how fast something changes at a single instant. If you know a car’s position at every moment, the derivative gives its speed, and the derivative of speed gives its acceleration. On a graph, the derivative is the steepness, or slope, of the curve at a point."],
["Integrals","An integral adds up infinitely many tiny pieces to find a total, such as the area under a curve, the distance traveled from a changing speed or the volume of an odd-shaped object. Leibniz introduced the stretched-S symbol ∫, from the Latin summa, meaning sum."],
["Limits","At the heart of calculus is the idea of a limit, the value a quantity approaches as something gets closer and closer to a point. In the 1800s mathematicians such as Augustin-Louis Cauchy and Karl Weierstrass made limits precise, putting calculus on a firm logical foundation."]],
k:[["Inventors","Isaac Newton and Gottfried Leibniz"],["Developed","1660s–1680s"],["Main branches","Differential and integral calculus"],["Key idea","The limit"],["Integral symbol","∫ (from Latin summa)"]],
d:[["c. 250 BCE","Archimedes’ method of exhaustion"],["c. 1400","Madhava’s infinite series"],["1665–66","Newton develops his fluxions"],["1684","Leibniz publishes differential calculus"],["1687","Newton’s Principia"],["1821","Cauchy’s rigorous treatment of limits"]]},
alg:{l:"Algebra is the branch of mathematics that uses letters and symbols to stand for numbers and relationships. By working with unknown quantities in equations, algebra lets us solve problems, describe patterns and write general rules, and it is the gateway to most advanced mathematics.",
s:[["Solving equations","An equation says that two expressions are equal, like a balanced scale. To solve 2x + 3 = 11, you do the same thing to both sides: subtract 3 to get 2x = 8, then divide by 2 to find x = 4. Al-jabr originally meant restoring, moving a quantity from one side to the other."],
["Early algebra","Babylonian clay tablets from about 1800 BCE show solutions to problems that we would now write as equations. The Greek mathematician Diophantus used symbols for unknowns around 250 CE and is sometimes called the father of algebra. Indian and Chinese mathematicians also solved complex equations."],
["Graphs and functions","Algebra can describe relationships between quantities. A function such as y = 2x + 1 gives one output for each input and can be drawn as a straight line on a graph. Equations such as y = x² make curves called parabolas, the path of a thrown ball."],
["Beyond numbers","In the 1800s mathematicians developed abstract algebra, which studies structures such as groups, rings and fields. Évariste Galois, who died at 20, used group theory to show which equations can be solved by formulas. Abstract algebra now underlies cryptography and physics."]],
k:[["Word origin","Arabic al-jabr, restoring"],["Key book","Al-Khwarizmi, c. 820"],["Equals sign","Robert Recorde, 1557"],["Letter x for unknowns","René Descartes, 1637"],["Father of algebra","Diophantus or al-Khwarizmi"]],
d:[["c. 1800 BCE","Babylonian algebra problems"],["c. 250 CE","Diophantus’ Arithmetica"],["c. 820","Al-Khwarizmi’s algebra book"],["1545","Cardano publishes cubic solutions"],["1557","Recorde introduces the equals sign"],["1832","Galois dies, leaving group theory"]]},
stats:{l:"Statistics is the science of collecting, organizing, analyzing and interpreting data. It helps governments plan, scientists test ideas, doctors judge treatments and businesses make decisions, and it teaches everyone to think carefully about the numbers they see in the news.",
s:[["Collecting data","Data can be collected through counts, measurements, surveys and experiments. A census counts every person in a country. National statistics offices conduct censuses, usually every five or ten years, and collect data on prices, jobs and the economy."],
["Showing data","Charts and graphs make data easier to understand. Bar charts compare groups, line graphs show change over time and pie charts show parts of a whole. But graphs can mislead, for example by starting an axis above zero to exaggerate differences."],
["Spread","Averages do not tell the whole story. Two classes might have the same mean score, but in one everyone scored close to the average while in the other scores ranged widely. The range and the standard deviation measure how spread out data are."],
["Testing ideas","Scientists use statistics to decide whether results are real or just chance. In a fair test of a new medicine, patients are randomly assigned to receive either the medicine or a placebo, a dummy treatment, and statistics show whether the difference is meaningful."]],
k:[["Averages","Mean, median, mode"],["Measure of spread","Standard deviation"],["Bell curve","Normal distribution"],["Typical poll size","About 1,000 people, accurate to about 3 points"],["Famous pioneer","Florence Nightingale"]],
d:[["1086","Domesday Book surveys England"],["1662","John Graunt studies London death records"],["1858","Nightingale’s rose diagrams"],["1790","The United States holds its first national census"],["1925","Ronald Fisher’s statistical methods"],["2013","The International Year of Statistics is celebrated"]]},
fib:{l:"The Fibonacci sequence is a famous list of numbers in which each number is the sum of the two before it. Linked to the golden ratio, a special number about 1.618, it appears in mathematics, nature and art, and it has fascinated people for more than 800 years.",
s:[["Leonardo of Pisa","Fibonacci was the son of a merchant who worked in North Africa, where young Leonardo learned Hindu–Arabic numerals. His Liber Abaci showed Europeans how to use these numerals for business, and it helped them replace Roman numerals. The rabbit puzzle was just one problem in the book."],
["Indian origins","The sequence was known in India centuries earlier. Indian scholars studying the rhythms of Sanskrit poetry, such as Virahanka around 700 CE and Hemachandra around 1150, counted patterns of long and short syllables and found the same numbers."],
["Spirals in nature","Sunflower seed heads often have 34 spirals one way and 55 the other, both Fibonacci numbers. Pinecones, pineapples and the arrangement of leaves around some stems show similar patterns. Packing seeds or leaves this way helps plants use space and sunlight efficiently."],
["The golden ratio","Dividing a line so that the whole is to the longer part as the longer part is to the shorter gives the golden ratio, called phi. A rectangle with sides in this ratio is called a golden rectangle. Claims that the Parthenon or the Mona Lisa were deliberately built on it are disputed."]],
k:[["Sequence starts","1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, 21"],["Rule","Each number is the sum of the two before"],["Golden ratio","About 1.618 (phi)"],["Book","Liber Abaci, 1202"],["Fibonacci Day","23 November (11/23)"]],
d:[["c. 200 BCE","Pingala studies poetic rhythms in India"],["c. 700","Virahanka describes the sequence"],["1202","Liber Abaci published"],["1611","Kepler links the sequence to the golden ratio"],["1877","Édouard Lucas names it the Fibonacci sequence"],["1963","Fibonacci Quarterly journal founded"]]},
pi:{l:"Pi, written with the Greek letter π, is one of the most famous numbers in mathematics. It is the ratio of any circle’s circumference to its diameter, a little more than 3, and it appears throughout mathematics, science and engineering.",
s:[["Ancient estimates","The Babylonians used 3⅛ for pi, and the Egyptian Rhind Papyrus implies about 3.16. Archimedes drew polygons with 96 sides inside and outside a circle to trap its value. In China, Zu Chongzhi found 355/113 around 480 CE, accurate to six decimal places."],
["Using pi","Pi lets us calculate a circle’s circumference, 2πr, and its area, πr², where r is the radius. It is used to work out the volume of cans, the orbits of satellites, the shape of waves and even the probability of events in statistics."],
["Squaring the circle","For more than 2,000 years mathematicians tried to construct a square with exactly the same area as a circle using only a compass and straightedge. In 1882 Ferdinand von Lindemann proved that pi is transcendental, which showed the task is impossible."],
["Pi records","People compete to memorize pi. Records exceed 70,000 digits recited from memory. Computers have calculated more than 100 trillion digits, mostly to test new hardware and software, since only a handful of digits are needed for practical use."]],
k:[["Value","3.14159265…"],["Symbol","π, popularized by Euler"],["Famous fraction","22/7 (approximate)"],["Proved irrational","1761, Lambert"],["Pi Day","14 March"]],
d:[["c. 1650 BCE","Rhind Papyrus estimate"],["c. 250 BCE","Archimedes’ bounds"],["c. 480","Zu Chongzhi’s 355/113"],["1706","William Jones first uses π"],["1882","Pi proved transcendental"],["1988","First Pi Day celebrated"]]},
infin:{l:"Infinity is the idea of something without any limit or end. It is not an ordinary number but a concept that has puzzled philosophers and mathematicians for thousands of years, and the study of infinity has led to some of the strangest and most beautiful results in mathematics.",
s:[["Potential and actual infinity","Aristotle distinguished potential infinity, a process that can always continue, such as counting, from actual infinity, a completed infinite collection. For centuries most thinkers accepted only the first. Modern mathematics, following Cantor, works freely with both."],
["Counting the infinite","Two sets are the same size if their members can be paired off one to one. Surprisingly, the even numbers can be paired with all the whole numbers, so there are just as many of each. Cantor proved that the real numbers cannot be paired with the whole numbers, so they form a larger infinity."],
["Infinite sums","Adding ½ + ¼ + ⅛ + … forever gives exactly 1. Such infinite series can add up to finite totals. Other series, such as 1 + ½ + ⅓ + ¼ + …, keep growing forever, even though the terms get smaller. Calculus is built on careful use of infinite processes."],
["Infinity in the universe","Scientists do not know whether space goes on forever. The observable universe has an edge, set by how far light has traveled since the Big Bang, about 46 billion light-years in every direction, but the whole universe may be much larger or even infinite."]],
k:[["Symbol","∞ (lemniscate)"],["Symbol introduced","John Wallis, 1655"],["Infinity pioneer","Georg Cantor"],["Smallest infinity","Aleph-null, the size of the whole numbers"],["Famous paradox","Zeno’s Achilles and the tortoise"]],
d:[["c. 450 BCE","Zeno’s paradoxes"],["c. 350 BCE","Aristotle on potential infinity"],["1638","Galileo’s paradox of the squares"],["1655","Wallis introduces ∞"],["1874","Cantor’s first paper on infinite sets"],["1924","Hilbert describes his infinite hotel"]]}
});

