// Longer, DK-style material for Economics and Society articles: a lead, extra sections, key facts and key dates.
window.DEEP=window.DEEP||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP,{
supdem:{l:"Supply and demand is the basic idea of how markets work. The price of almost anything, from rice and fish to concert tickets and electricity, is shaped by how much of it people want to buy and how much sellers are willing to offer. Where the two meet, a market price is set.",
s:[["The law of demand","As the price of a good rises, people usually buy less of it, and as the price falls, they buy more. If fish becomes expensive, families may buy chicken instead. Drawn on a graph with price going up and quantity across, this relationship forms a downward-sloping demand curve."],
["The law of supply","Sellers usually offer more of a good when its price is high, because producing it becomes more profitable. If mango prices rise, farmers may plant more mango trees. This gives an upward-sloping supply curve. Where supply and demand curves cross is the equilibrium price."],
["Shortages and surpluses","If a price is set below the equilibrium, people want more than is available, causing a shortage and queues. If a price is set above it, sellers cannot sell everything, causing a surplus. Governments sometimes set price ceilings during emergencies, such as after typhoons, to keep essentials affordable."],
["Elasticity","Elasticity measures how much demand changes when prices change. Demand for necessities such as rice, salt and medicine is inelastic, meaning people keep buying even if prices rise. Demand for luxuries, such as travel or jewelry, is elastic and falls sharply when prices rise."]],
k:[["Key thinker","Adam Smith (1776)"],["Graph pioneer","Alfred Marshall (1890)"],["Meeting point","Equilibrium price"],["Too little supply","Shortage"],["Too much supply","Surplus"]],
d:[["1776","Smith’s The Wealth of Nations"],["1838","Cournot draws early demand curves"],["1890","Marshall’s Principles of Economics"],["1936","Keynes’s General Theory"],["1970s","Oil shocks show supply’s power"],["2020","Pandemic disrupts supply chains"]]},
money:{l:"Money is anything widely accepted in exchange for goods and services. From shells and metal coins to paper notes and digital payments, money makes trade far easier than barter, and banks keep it safe, lend it out and move it around the economy.",
s:[["Before money","In barter, people swap goods directly, such as fish for grain. But barter only works if each person wants what the other has. Many societies used commodity money, such as shells, salt, cattle or cacao beans, and gold, beads and porcelain were used in trade across Southeast Asia."],
["Coins and notes","Coins of gold, silver and copper were valuable for their metal. Paper money began in China as receipts for coins and was later printed by the government. Today most money is fiat money, valuable because governments declare it legal tender and people trust it, not because it is backed by gold."],
["How banks work","Banks take deposits and pay interest, then lend much of that money to people and businesses, charging higher interest on loans. This lets savings fund new homes, farms and factories. Deposit insurance, first introduced nationally in the United States in 1934, protects savers if a bank fails."],
["Currencies of the world","There are about 180 currencies in use. The US dollar is the most widely used in world trade and savings, followed by the euro, shared by 21 European Union countries. Many currency names have long histories: the peso, used in Mexico, the Philippines and much of Latin America, descends from the Spanish silver dollar, and the rupee from the silver rupiya of 16th-century India."]],
k:[["Functions","Medium of exchange, store of value, unit of account"],["First coins","Lydia, c. 600 BCE"],["First paper money","China (Song dynasty)"],["Oldest central bank","Sveriges Riksbank, Sweden, 1668"],["Most traded currency","US dollar"]],
d:[["c. 600 BCE","First coins in Lydia"],["c. 1024","Song government issues paper money"],["1694","Bank of England founded"],["1694","The Bank of England is founded"],["1971","US ends dollar link to gold"],["2002","Euro banknotes and coins enter circulation"]]},
trade:{l:"Trade is the buying and selling of goods and services, and globalization is the growing connection of the world’s economies through trade, investment, travel and communication. Today a single phone may contain parts from dozens of countries, assembled in one place and sold around the world.",
s:[["Ancient trade","People have traded for thousands of years. Obsidian, a volcanic glass, was traded across long distances in the Stone Age. Before Europeans arrived in Asia, merchants traded across the Indian Ocean and South China Sea, exchanging gold, pearls, spices and forest products for porcelain and silk."],
["Exports and imports","Exports are goods and services sold to other countries, and imports are those bought from abroad. The balance of trade is the difference between them. Saudi Arabia exports mainly oil, Chile copper and Bangladesh clothing, while countries such as Japan import much of their fuel and food."],
["Trade agreements","Countries sign agreements to lower tariffs and other barriers. The European Union is a single market, ASEAN countries have a free trade area, and the Regional Comprehensive Economic Partnership, signed by 15 Asia-Pacific countries in 2020, is one of the largest trade deals in the world. The World Trade Organization settles trade disputes."],
["Debates about globalization","Supporters say globalization lowers prices, spreads technology and lifts millions out of poverty. Critics say it can cost jobs in some places, widen inequality and harm the environment through long-distance shipping. The COVID-19 pandemic showed how fragile global supply chains can be."]],
k:[["Key theory","Comparative advantage (Ricardo, 1817)"],["Global trade body","World Trade Organization (1995)"],["Regional bloc","ASEAN Free Trade Area (1992)"],["Container shipping began","1956"],["Largest exporter","China"]],
d:[["1565–1815","Manila galleon trade links Asia and the Americas"],["1817","Ricardo explains comparative advantage"],["1947","General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade"],["1956","First container ship sails"],["1995","World Trade Organization founded"],["2001","China joins the World Trade Organization"]]},
inflation:{l:"Inflation is the rate at which the general level of prices rises over time. When inflation is high, money loses its value quickly, so people can buy less with the same amount. Keeping inflation low and stable is one of the main jobs of central banks.",
s:[["Measuring inflation","Statisticians track the prices of a basket of goods and services that typical households buy, such as food, fuel, rent and transport fares. The change in the cost of the basket over a year is the inflation rate. National statistics offices publish a consumer price index, usually every month."],
["Causes","Inflation can happen when demand grows faster than the economy can produce, called demand-pull inflation, or when costs such as oil and wages rise, called cost-push inflation. Printing too much money can cause very high inflation. In many poorer countries, food and fuel prices strongly affect inflation."],
["Who is hurt","Inflation hurts people on fixed incomes and savers, since their money buys less. Poor families, who spend a large share of income on food, feel price rises most. Borrowers can benefit, because they repay loans with money worth less than when they borrowed."],
["Deflation","Falling prices, called deflation, might sound good, but it can be harmful. People delay buying in the hope of lower prices, businesses earn less and cut jobs, and debts become harder to repay. Japan struggled with deflation for years from the 1990s."]],
k:[["Measure","Consumer price index (CPI)"],["Typical target","About 2% in many rich countries"],["Opposite","Deflation"],["Extreme form","Hyperinflation"],["Main tool against it","Raising interest rates"]],
d:[["1500s","Silver from the Americas fuels a European price revolution"],["1923","German hyperinflation"],["1946","Hungary suffers the worst recorded hyperinflation"],["1970s","Oil shocks cause high inflation worldwide"],["1990","New Zealand becomes the first country to adopt an inflation target"],["2022","Global inflation surge after the pandemic"]]},
gdp:{l:"Gross domestic product, or GDP, is the most widely used measure of the size of an economy. It adds up the value of everything produced within a country in a given period, and economists, governments and news reports use it to judge whether an economy is growing or shrinking.",
s:[["Three ways to measure","GDP can be measured in three ways that should give the same result: by adding up everything produced, by adding up all the money people and businesses earn, or by adding up all spending, by households, businesses, the government and foreigners buying exports, minus imports."],
["Real and nominal","Nominal GDP uses current prices, so it rises with inflation even if nothing more is produced. Real GDP removes the effect of price changes, showing whether the economy actually produces more. Comparisons between countries often adjust for differences in living costs, called purchasing power parity."],
["Recessions","When real GDP falls for two quarters in a row, it is often called a recession. In 2020 the world economy shrank by about 3% because of pandemic lockdowns, the worst drop since World War II, and some countries, such as the United Kingdom and Spain, shrank by about 10% before rebounding."],
["Beyond GDP","Because GDP misses much of what makes life good, other measures have been created. The Human Development Index combines income, health and education. Bhutan promotes Gross National Happiness, and some countries track green GDP, which subtracts environmental damage."]],
k:[["Measures","Value of all goods and services produced"],["Developed by","Simon Kuznets, 1930s"],["Adjusted for inflation","Real GDP"],["Largest economy","United States"],["Alternative measure","Human Development Index"]],
d:[["1934","Kuznets presents national income estimates"],["1944","Bretton Woods makes GDP a standard measure"],["1953","UN System of National Accounts"],["1990","Human Development Index introduced"],["2008","Global financial crisis causes recessions"],["2020","The world economy shrinks by about 3% during the pandemic"]]},
stockm:{l:"A stock market is where people buy and sell shares, small pieces of ownership in companies. By selling shares, companies raise money to grow, and investors can share in their profits. Stock markets are watched closely as signs of how an economy is doing.",
s:[["What a share is","Owning a share means owning a small part of a company. If the company does well, the share price may rise, and shareholders may receive part of the profits, called dividends. If it does badly, the price may fall. Shareholders can vote on some company decisions."],
["How trading works","Buyers and sellers place orders through brokers, and prices change as supply and demand shift. Trading once happened on crowded floors, with traders shouting and using hand signals. Today almost all trading is electronic, and computers can make trades in fractions of a second."],
["Risk and reward","Shares can grow in value over time, but they can also lose value quickly. Experts advise investors to spread their money across many companies, called diversification, and to invest for the long term. Index funds, which buy all the shares in an index, are a popular low-cost option."],
["Exchanges around the world","The New York Stock Exchange and Nasdaq are the largest stock markets. Others include the exchanges of Shanghai, Tokyo, Hong Kong, London, Mumbai and Frankfurt. Most trading is now electronic, done by computers in fractions of a second, and many people invest through online brokers with small amounts."]],
k:[["First stock exchange","Amsterdam, 1602"],["Largest exchange","New York Stock Exchange"],["Largest exchange","New York Stock Exchange"],["Famous Japanese index","Nikkei 225"],["Rising market","Bull market"]],
d:[["1602","Amsterdam exchange begins"],["1792","Buttonwood Agreement founds the NYSE’s forerunner"],["1971","Nasdaq, the first electronic stock market, opens"],["1929","Wall Street Crash"],["1987","Black Monday: stock markets crash around the world"],["2008","Global financial crisis"]]},
insur:{l:"Insurance is a way of protecting people and businesses against financial loss. By paying regular amounts called premiums into a shared pool, policyholders can be compensated if something bad happens, such as illness, accident, fire, theft or a natural disaster.",
s:[["Ancient roots","Babylonian traders around 1750 BCE arranged loans for voyages that did not have to be repaid if the ship was lost, an idea recorded in the Code of Hammurabi. Chinese merchants spread their goods across many boats so that one wreck would not ruin anyone."],
["Kinds of insurance","Life insurance pays money to a family when the insured person dies. Health insurance helps pay medical bills. Property insurance covers homes and businesses against fire, flood and theft, and car insurance covers accidents. Many countries provide national health insurance."],
["Fire and the modern industry","After the Great Fire of London in 1666 destroyed more than 13,000 houses, the first fire insurance companies were set up. Some had their own fire brigades and marked insured buildings with metal badges called fire marks."],
["Insuring against disasters","Countries prone to disasters use insurance to recover faster. Caribbean and Pacific nations share risk through regional catastrophe insurance pools, and many governments buy cover that pays out after strong hurricanes, typhoons and earthquakes. Crop insurance helps farmers recover from lost harvests."]],
k:[["Payment","Premium"],["Claim payout","Indemnity or benefit"],["Risk experts","Actuaries"],["Famous market","Lloyd’s of London"],["First national health insurance","Germany, 1883"]],
d:[["c. 1750 BCE","Code of Hammurabi mentions risk-sharing loans"],["1666","Great Fire of London"],["1688","Lloyd’s coffee house opens"],["1762","First modern life insurer, the Equitable, in London"],["1995","PhilHealth created"],["2007","The Caribbean Catastrophe Risk Insurance Facility is created"]]},
urban:{l:"Urbanization is the growth of towns and cities as people move from the countryside in search of jobs, education and opportunity. Cities are centers of trade, culture and new ideas, but rapid growth also brings challenges such as crowding, traffic and pollution.",
s:[["The first cities","The first cities grew up about 5,000 to 6,000 years ago in river valleys, such as Uruk in Mesopotamia, Mohenjo-daro in the Indus Valley and cities along the Nile. Farming surpluses allowed people to live in towns and work as craftsmen, priests and traders."],
["The urban explosion","In 1800 only about 3% of people lived in cities. Industrialization drew millions to factory towns in Europe and America, and since 1950 cities in Asia, Africa and Latin America have grown fastest. By 2050 about two thirds of humanity is expected to live in urban areas."],
["Informal settlements","In many fast-growing cities, people who cannot afford housing build homes on unused land, forming informal settlements, sometimes called slums. About a billion people live in them, in places such as Mumbai’s Dharavi, Nairobi’s Kibera and the favelas of Rio de Janeiro. Upgrading these areas with water, sanitation and secure land rights improves lives."],
["Smart and green cities","Planners design cities that are easier to walk and cycle, with parks, trees and good public transport. New Clark City in Tarlac is being built as a planned, disaster-resilient green city. Smart city technology uses sensors and data to manage traffic, energy and waste."]],
k:[["Urban share (world)","More than 55%"],["Largest urban area","Jakarta, about 42 million (UN, 2025)"],["Megacities","More than 30 cities of over 10 million people"],["Megacity","More than 10 million people"],["Urban share by 2050","About 68% (projected)"]],
d:[["c. 3500 BCE","Uruk among the first cities"],["1800","About 3% of people live in cities"],["1950","New York and Tokyo are the first megacities"],["1960","Brasília, a planned capital, is inaugurated in Brazil"],["2007","Half of humanity becomes urban"],["2016","New Clark City project launched"]]},
taxes:{l:"Taxes are payments that people and businesses are required to make to the government. They fund public services such as schools, hospitals, roads, police and defense, and the way taxes are designed affects fairness, economic growth and what kind of society a country builds.",
s:[["Direct and indirect","Direct taxes, such as income tax, are paid straight to the government by the person who owes them. Indirect taxes, such as value-added tax (VAT), are included in the price of goods and services and passed on to the government by sellers. VAT rates range from 5 percent in Taiwan to 25 percent or more in Scandinavia and 27 percent in Hungary."],
["Progressive and flat","A progressive tax takes a higher percentage from people with higher incomes. A flat tax charges everyone the same rate. Most countries’ income taxes are progressive, often letting people with the lowest incomes pay nothing, while some, such as Estonia, use a flat rate."],
["Tribute in the colony","Taxes have often sparked revolts. American colonists protested taxes passed by a British parliament in which they had no say, crying no taxation without representation. Heavy taxes on peasants helped cause the French Revolution, Gandhi’s Salt March of 1930 defied the British salt tax in India, and in 1896 Filipino revolutionaries tore up their cedulas, the tax certificates of Spanish rule."],
["Where taxes go","Each year the government prepares a national budget, approved by Congress, deciding how tax money is spent. Large shares go to education, infrastructure, health, social welfare, defense and paying interest on debt. Citizens can follow the budget and file their taxes with the Bureau of Internal Revenue."]],
k:[["Oldest income tax","Britain, 1799"],["Highest VAT rate","Hungary, 27%"],["Customs agency","Bureau of Customs"],["Income tax type","Progressive"],["Annual filing deadline","15 April"]],
d:[["c. 3000 BCE","Egyptian pharaohs collect grain taxes"],["1799","Britain introduces income tax"],["1913","The United States introduces a permanent federal income tax"],["1904","Bureau of Internal Revenue created"],["1954","France introduces value-added tax"],["2018","TRAIN tax reform takes effect"]]},
labor:{l:"Labor is the work people do to produce goods and services, and it is one of the main foundations of any economy. How work is organized, rewarded and protected has changed enormously over history, from farming and craft workshops to factories, offices and online platforms.",
s:[["Division of labor","Adam Smith described how a pin factory could make far more pins when each worker did one small step rather than the whole job. This division of labor raised productivity enormously. Henry Ford’s moving assembly line of 1913 applied the same idea to car making."],
["Unions and rights","Workers formed trade unions to bargain together for better pay, shorter hours and safer conditions. The eight-hour day became a major goal of labor movements around the world. Unions grew in Britain from the early 1800s and spread to Europe, the Americas, Asia and Africa."],
["The Labor Code","Labor laws set rules on minimum wages, working hours, holidays, safety and the right to form unions. The International Labour Organization, founded in 1919 and now a UN agency, sets international labor standards that most countries have signed."],
["Work abroad and the future","About 170 million people work in countries other than their own, many in the Gulf states, Europe and North America. Meanwhile automation and artificial intelligence are changing many jobs. Experts say lifelong learning and new skills will help workers adapt."]],
k:[["Global labor body","International Labour Organization (1919)"],["Workers’ Day","1 May"],["International body","International Labour Organization (1919)"],["First national minimum wage","New Zealand, 1894"],["Workers abroad","About 170 million"]],
d:[["1776","Smith describes the division of labor"],["1886","Haymarket affair in Chicago"],["1919","The International Labour Organization is founded"],["1913","Ford’s moving assembly line"],["1919","International Labour Organization founded"],["1980","Poland’s Solidarity union is founded"]]}
});

