// Longer, DK-style material for Food and Agriculture articles: a lead, extra sections, key facts and key dates.
window.DEEP=window.DEEP||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP,{
rice:{l:"Rice is the seed of a grass that has fed more people than any other crop in history. Grown in flooded paddies and on dry hillsides from Asia to Africa and the Americas, it is the staple food of more than half the world.",
s:[["Growing rice","Most rice grows in paddies, flat fields flooded with a few centimeters of water, which helps control weeds. Farmers raise seedlings in nursery beds and transplant them by hand or machine. After three to six months the grain ripens and turns golden, and it is harvested, threshed, dried and milled."],
["From grain to plate","Milling removes the husk to give brown rice, and polishing removes the bran to give white rice, which keeps longer but loses some vitamins. Rice is boiled or steamed, fried as sinangag, ground into flour for puto and bibingka, or fermented to make drinks and vinegar."],
["Rice around the world","China and India grow more than half of the world’s rice, followed by Bangladesh, Indonesia, Vietnam and Thailand. India is the largest exporter. In Bangladesh and Myanmar, people eat well over 150 kg of rice per person a year, while many Western countries eat only a few kilograms. Rice is also a staple in West Africa, Madagascar and much of Latin America."],
["Golden rice and climate","Scientists developed golden rice, enriched with beta-carotene to fight vitamin A deficiency, and in 2021 the Philippines became the first country to approve it for planting. Researchers also breed rice that can survive floods, drought and salty water as the climate changes."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Oryza sativa (Asian rice)"],["Domesticated","Yangtze region, about 9,000 years ago"],["Largest producers","China and India"],["Largest exporter","India"],["Research institute","IRRI, Los Baños (1960)"]],
d:[["c. 7000 BCE","Rice domesticated in China"],["c. 1000 BCE","African rice is domesticated in the Niger delta (estimated)"],["1960","IRRI founded"],["1966","IR8 miracle rice released"],["1985","PhilRice established"],["2008","A world rice price crisis hits importing countries"]]},
agri:{l:"The Agricultural Revolution, also called the Neolithic Revolution, was the change from hunting and gathering to farming and herding that began about 12,000 years ago. It was one of the most important turning points in human history, leading to villages, cities, writing and civilization itself.",
s:[["Why farming began","After the last ice age, about 11,700 years ago, the climate became warmer and more stable. In places such as the hills of the Fertile Crescent, wild wheat and barley grew in large stands. People began harvesting, storing and planting them, and over centuries they settled down near their fields."],
["Many cradles","Farming began independently in at least several regions. People in the Fertile Crescent grew wheat, barley, lentils and peas; in China, rice and millet; in Mesoamerica, maize, beans and squash; in the Andes, potatoes; and in New Guinea, taro and bananas. Later, African farmers domesticated sorghum and yams."],
["Taming animals","Dogs were domesticated first, from wolves. Sheep and goats followed about 11,000 years ago, then cattle and pigs. Animals provided meat, milk, wool and leather, and later pulled plows and carts. Chickens were domesticated in Southeast Asia, and the water buffalo, or carabao, became the farmer’s partner across Asia."],
["Costs and benefits","Farming supported far more people, but early farmers often worked harder than hunter-gatherers, had less varied diets and suffered new diseases caught from animals and crowded villages. Over time, though, surplus food allowed trade, crafts, writing and governments to develop."]],
k:[["Began","About 12,000 years ago"],["Earliest center","Fertile Crescent, Middle East"],["First crops","Wheat, barley, lentils, peas"],["First farm animals","Sheep and goats"],["Also called","Neolithic Revolution"]],
d:[["c. 10,000 BCE","Farming begins in the Fertile Crescent"],["c. 9000 BCE","Sheep and goats domesticated"],["c. 7000 BCE","Rice farming in China; maize in Mexico"],["c. 7000 BCE","Çatalhöyük, an early farming town"],["c. 4000 BCE","The plow is introduced"],["1960s","Green Revolution boosts harvests"]]},
coffee:{l:"Coffee and tea are the world’s most popular hot drinks. Made from roasted coffee beans and the dried leaves of the tea plant, they are enjoyed by billions of people every day, and their cultivation and trade have shaped economies, cultures and empires.",
s:[["From cherry to cup","Coffee beans are the seeds of red fruits called cherries, which grow on shrubs in the tropics. After picking, the seeds are removed, dried, roasted and ground. Roasting turns them brown and creates hundreds of flavor compounds. Brewing with hot water extracts the flavor and caffeine."],
["Coffee rust and the spread of coffee","Coffee spread from Ethiopia to Yemen in the 1400s and from there across the Arab world, to Europe in the 1600s and to the Americas in the 1700s. In the 1870s a fungal disease called coffee rust destroyed the plantations of Ceylon, now Sri Lanka, which switched to tea, and it later spread to many other regions. Brazil became the world’s largest producer in the 1800s and still is."],
["The story of tea","Chinese legend says tea was discovered when leaves blew into Emperor Shennong’s boiling water. Tea spread to Japan, where it became part of a refined ceremony, and then to Europe and Britain. The British grew tea in India and Sri Lanka, which remain huge producers."],
["Kinds of tea","All true teas come from the Camellia sinensis plant. Green tea is dried quickly, while black tea is left to oxidize, turning dark. Oolong is in between. Herbal drinks, such as mint or chamomile infusions, are not true teas but are often called teas."]],
k:[["Coffee species","Arabica, robusta, liberica, excelsa"],["Top coffee producer","Brazil"],["Tea plant","Camellia sinensis"],["Top tea producer","China"],["Barako bean","Liberica"]],
d:[["c. 2737 BCE","Legendary discovery of tea in China"],["c. 1450","Coffee drinking in Yemeni monasteries"],["1554","Coffeehouses open in Istanbul"],["1723","Coffee plants reach the Caribbean"],["1869","Coffee rust devastates plantations in Ceylon"],["1971","Starbucks opens in Seattle"]]},
wheat:{l:"Wheat is a cereal grass whose grains are ground into flour for bread, noodles, pasta, cakes and pastries. One of the first plants ever farmed, it is now grown on more land than any other food crop and supplies about a fifth of the calories eaten by humanity.",
s:[["Inside a grain","A wheat grain has three parts. The bran is the fiber-rich outer skin, the germ is the part that would sprout, and the endosperm is the starchy food store. White flour comes mostly from the endosperm, while whole wheat flour includes all three parts and has more fiber and vitamins."],
["Gluten","Wheat contains proteins that form gluten when mixed with water. Gluten makes dough stretchy and traps gas from yeast, so bread rises. A small number of people have celiac disease and must avoid gluten, which damages their intestines."],
["Bread around the world","Nearly every culture has a bread. Flatbreads such as Indian roti and naan, Mexican flour tortillas and Middle Eastern pita are cooked on hot surfaces, while loaves such as French baguettes and German rye breads rely on yeast or sourdough. Bread has been a staple food for thousands of years."],
["Feeding the world","Wheat is grown from Canada to Argentina and from Ukraine to Australia. Because so many countries depend on imports, wars or droughts in major producing regions can raise bread prices around the world, as happened after Russia’s invasion of Ukraine in 2022."]],
k:[["First farmed","About 10,000 years ago, Fertile Crescent"],["Share of human calories","About 20%"],["Main parts of a grain","Bran, germ, endosperm"],["Top producers","China, India, Russia"],["Famous wheat foods","Bread, pasta, couscous"]],
d:[["c. 9600 BCE","Einkorn and emmer farmed"],["c. 3000 BCE","Egyptians bake leavened bread"],["1786","Steam-powered Albion Mills open in London"],["1880s","Roller mills make white flour cheap"],["1960s","High-yield wheat in the Green Revolution"],["2022","War in Ukraine raises grain prices"]]},
spice:{l:"Spices are dried seeds, bark, roots, fruits or flowers that add flavor, color and aroma to food. For thousands of years they were among the most valuable goods on Earth, and the search for them drove voyages of exploration that reshaped the world.",
s:[["Spices of the world","Cinnamon is the inner bark of trees from Sri Lanka and southern China. Cloves are dried flower buds, and nutmeg and mace come from the same fruit. Saffron, the world’s most expensive spice, is the dried threads of a crocus flower. Turmeric, ginger and chili are also widely used."],
["Ancient trade","Egyptians used spices in embalming, and Roman cooks prized pepper so much that the Goths demanded about 1,350 kg of it as part of a ransom for Rome in 408 CE. Arab and Indian merchants controlled the trade routes and kept the sources secret, telling tales of spices guarded by fierce birds."],
["The race for the Spice Islands","Portugal reached the Maluku Islands in 1512, and Magellan’s expedition, sailing west for Spain, sought another route there. The Dutch later seized control, and in 1667 they traded the island of Manhattan to England in exchange for the tiny nutmeg island of Run."],
["Chili comes to Asia","Chili peppers came from the Americas and spread through Asia and Africa after the 1500s, carried partly by the Manila galleons. Within a century they had become essential in the cooking of India, Thailand and Korea. Hungarian paprika and Mexican salsas are other famous chili products."]],
k:[["Most traded spice","Black pepper"],["Most expensive","Saffron"],["Original Spice Islands","Maluku, Indonesia"],["Major trading company","Dutch East India Company (1602)"],["Famous chili product","Hungarian paprika"]],
d:[["c. 1500 BCE","Egyptians use cinnamon"],["408 CE","Pepper demanded as ransom for Rome"],["1498","Da Gama reaches India by sea"],["1512","Portuguese reach the Spice Islands"],["1602","Dutch East India Company founded"],["1667","Run traded for Manhattan"]]},
choco:{l:"Chocolate is made from the seeds of the cacao tree, a plant native to the rainforests of Central and South America. First enjoyed as a bitter drink by ancient Mesoamerican peoples, it became one of the world’s favorite sweet treats.",
s:[["Food of the gods","Cacao’s scientific name, Theobroma, means food of the gods. The Olmec may have been the first to use it more than 3,000 years ago. The Maya and Aztecs mixed ground cacao with water, chili and spices and poured it from height to make it frothy. Aztec emperors were said to drink it from golden cups."],
["From bean to bar","Cacao pods are cut open, and the beans and pulp are left to ferment for several days, which develops the chocolate flavor. The beans are dried, roasted and cracked to give nibs, which are ground into cocoa liquor. This is pressed into cocoa butter and cocoa powder or mixed with sugar and milk to make chocolate."],
["Cacao around the world","Cacao grows only in a narrow band within about 20 degrees of the equator. Spanish and Portuguese traders carried it from Mexico to the Philippines, Africa and the Caribbean, and today West Africa leads production, followed by countries including Indonesia, Ecuador and Brazil. In Mexico, hot chocolate is still made by whisking ground cacao with water, while fine-flavor cacao from Ecuador and Venezuela is prized by makers of bean-to-bar chocolate."],
["Bittersweet problems","Most cocoa is grown by small farmers in West Africa who earn very little, and child labor has been found on many farms. Diseases and climate change threaten harvests. Fair trade and sustainability programs aim to improve farmers’ incomes and protect forests."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Theobroma cacao"],["Native region","Central and South America"],["Top producer","Ivory Coast"],["Beans per pod","20 to 60"],["Top producers","Ivory Coast and Ghana"]],
d:[["c. 1900 BCE","Earliest traces of cacao use in Mexico"],["1528","Cortés brings cacao to Spain (traditional)"],["1600s","Drinking chocolate becomes fashionable in European courts"],["1828","Van Houten’s cocoa press"],["1847","First chocolate bar"],["1875","Milk chocolate invented"]]},
fish:{l:"Fishing is the catching of fish and other water animals, and aquaculture is the farming of them in ponds, cages and tanks. Together they provide food and jobs for hundreds of millions of people, especially in coastal and island nations.",
s:[["Ways to fish","Fishers use hooks and lines, nets, traps and spears. Small boats, such as outrigger canoes in Asia and the Pacific and pirogues in West Africa, fish close to shore. Large trawlers drag huge nets and can stay at sea for weeks, freezing their catch on board. Some methods catch unwanted animals, called bycatch."],
["Destructive fishing","Dynamite and cyanide fishing, still used on some coral reefs, kill coral and young fish. They are illegal in most countries. Overfishing has reduced catches in many areas, so governments set quotas and closed seasons to let fish breed. The collapse of the Atlantic cod off Canada in 1992 showed what happens when fishing goes too far."],
["Ponds, cages and seaweed","Fish farms raise carp, tilapia, salmon, catfish, milkfish and shrimp in ponds, cages and tanks. Seaweed farming is also important, especially in China, Indonesia and the Philippines, which produce carrageenan and other seaweed extracts used in foods and cosmetics. Farming must be managed carefully to avoid pollution and mangrove loss."],
["Marine protected areas","When fishing is banned in part of the sea, fish grow larger and more numerous and spill over into nearby fishing grounds. Thousands of marine protected areas now exist, from huge reserves around remote Pacific islands to small community-run sanctuaries, such as Apo Island in the Philippines."]],
k:[["Fish’s share of animal protein","About 15% worldwide"],["Aquaculture share","About half of fish eaten"],["Largest producer","China"],["Largest farmed salmon producer","Norway"],["National fish (popular)","Bangus (milkfish)"]],
d:[["c. 21,000 BCE","Some of the oldest known fish hooks made in Timor"],["1880s","Steam trawlers appear"],["1950s","Industrial fishing expands worldwide"],["1982","UN Law of the Sea sets fishing zones"],["1992","The Atlantic cod fishery off Newfoundland collapses"],["2022","Aquaculture overtakes wild capture"]]},
potato:{l:"The potato is a starchy underground tuber that is one of the world’s most important food crops. First grown by farmers in the Andes Mountains of South America, it spread around the globe and helped feed growing populations in Europe and beyond.",
s:[["Thousands of varieties","Andean farmers developed more than 4,000 varieties of potato, in colors from yellow and red to purple and blue, suited to different altitudes and soils. The International Potato Center in Lima, Peru, keeps a gene bank of these varieties to protect them for the future."],
["Suspicion in Europe","When potatoes first arrived in Europe, many people distrusted them, since they belong to the nightshade family, which includes poisonous plants. Rulers such as Frederick the Great of Prussia encouraged farmers to plant them, and they became cheap, filling food for workers."],
["Growing potatoes","Potatoes are grown from pieces of tuber, called seed potatoes, rather than seeds. They need cool weather. In the tropics they are grown mainly in cool highlands."],
["Nutrition","Potatoes provide carbohydrates, vitamin C, potassium and fiber, especially when eaten with their skins. Green parts of potatoes contain solanine, a mildly toxic substance, so green or sprouted parts should be cut away."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Solanum tuberosum"],["Origin","Andes, about 8,000 years ago"],["Varieties","More than 4,000"],["Top producer","China"],["Home region","The Andes of South America"]],
d:[["c. 6000 BCE","Potatoes domesticated in the Andes"],["c. 1570","Potatoes reach Spain"],["1756","Frederick the Great orders potatoes planted"],["1845","Irish potato famine begins"],["1971","International Potato Center founded"],["2008","International Year of the Potato"]]},
sugar:{l:"Sugar is a sweet substance made mainly from sugarcane and sugar beet. Once a rare luxury, it became one of the world’s most traded goods, with a history tied to empire, slavery and industry, and today it is found in countless foods and drinks.",
s:[["From cane to crystals","Sugarcane is a tall tropical grass with sweet, juicy stalks. At a mill, the stalks are crushed to squeeze out the juice, which is boiled to remove water until crystals form. Spinning separates the crystals from the dark syrup called molasses. Raw sugar is brown, and refining makes it white."],
["White gold","In medieval Europe, sugar was so costly it was called white gold and kept under lock and key. Arab farmers spread sugarcane across the Mediterranean, and Columbus brought it to the Caribbean in 1493. Millions of enslaved Africans were forced to work on sugar plantations."],
["Plantations and workers","In the 1800s and 1900s huge sugar estates developed in places such as Cuba, Hawaii, Queensland and Fiji, often relying on enslaved or indentured workers who were poorly paid. Plantation owners grew rich, while workers lived in poverty. Falling sugar prices could bring hardship to whole regions."],
["Sugar beets","In 1747 the German chemist Andreas Marggraf discovered that beets contain sugar. Napoleon encouraged beet sugar when the British blockade cut off cane sugar from the Caribbean. Today about a fifth of the world’s sugar comes from beets grown in cooler climates."]],
k:[["Main sources","Sugarcane (about 80%) and sugar beet"],["Cane domesticated","New Guinea, about 8,000 years ago"],["Top producers","Brazil and India"],["Top producers","Brazil and India"],["By-product","Molasses"]],
d:[["c. 6000 BCE","Sugarcane domesticated in New Guinea"],["c. 350 CE","Sugar crystals made in India"],["1493","Columbus brings cane to the Caribbean"],["1747","Sugar found in beets"],["1747","Andreas Marggraf discovers sugar in beets"],["1980s","Falling world prices hit many cane-growing regions"]]},
maize:{l:"Maize, also called corn, is a cereal grain first domesticated in Mexico. It became the foundation of the great civilizations of the Americas and is now the most widely produced grain in the world, used for food, animal feed, fuel and many industrial products.",
s:[["From teosinte to corn","Teosinte has tiny ears with only a few hard kernels. Over thousands of years, farmers selected plants with bigger ears and softer kernels, creating maize. It cannot survive without human help, because its kernels stay tightly wrapped in the husk and cannot scatter on their own."],
["The three sisters","Native American farmers often planted maize, beans and squash together, called the three sisters. Maize stalks support the climbing beans, beans add nitrogen to the soil and squash leaves shade out weeds. Together they provide a balanced diet."],
["Nixtamalization","Mesoamerican cooks soaked maize in water with lime or ashes, a process called nixtamalization, before grinding it for tortillas and tamales. This releases niacin, a B vitamin. When maize spread to Europe without this method, people who ate little else developed the disease pellagra."],
["Maize around the world","Maize reached Europe, Africa and Asia in the 1500s and 1600s, carried by Spanish and Portuguese traders, and quickly became a staple. In Mexico it is eaten as tortillas and tamales, in Italy as polenta, and in southern Africa as a thick porridge called pap or sadza. Much of the maize grown in the United States, China and Brazil, the world’s top producers, is used as animal feed."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Zea mays"],["Origin","Southern Mexico, about 9,000 years ago"],["Wild ancestor","Teosinte"],["Top producer","United States"],["World’s most produced grain","Maize"]],
d:[["c. 7000 BCE","Maize domesticated in Mexico"],["1493","Columbus brings maize to Europe"],["1600s","Maize spreads across Africa and Asia"],["1930s","Hybrid corn transforms US farming"],["1996","Genetically modified corn first planted"],["2002","Genetically modified maize is grown in a growing number of countries"]]}
});

